<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Town Square</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Look at the Traditional and Modern Building Materials.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe changes in places. Interactive: Talk about preferences and familiar topics in a guided class discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe and compare changes in local places on a map (e.g., "The market used to be small, but now it is larger.").
                Can discuss preferences for places in the town square (e.g., "I like La Fondita de Lucia. It has good Panamanian food. What do you think?").
                Can ask and answer questions about location preferences (e.g., "Where is the library? Do you like it there?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large image of a historic town square with both traditional (e.g., old church, stone monument, wooden roof) and modern elements (e.g., new pedestrian bridge, glass building, traffic light). Students are asked to point and name familiar objects using the target vocabulary. For example, "What do you see here? Is this a traditional building or a modern building?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing locations and expressing preferences using a simple map of a town square on the board. The teacher points and says, "There is a statue in the middle of the plaza. I like the statue because it is historic. The library is next to the park. What do you think?" The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /s/ in words like "statue" and "street."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using questions:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use 'There is' for one thing or many things?" (Expected answer: One thing.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'The park is *next to* the library,' are they close or far?" (Expected answer: Close.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, simplified map of a town square with 5-7 key locations (e.g., market, library, school, fountain, statue, modern building, traditional building) clearly labeled. The map is displayed digitally or as a handout.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will learn to describe changes in places and talk about preferences for familiar topics in a guided class discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher points to different locations on the map and explicitly names them, emphasizing new vocabulary (e.g., "This is the town hall," "Here is a modern building, and there is a traditional market across from it."). The teacher uses "There is/are" and prepositions of place. The teacher also introduces phrases for changes, such as "The market used to be small, but now it is larger," connecting to the theme of traditional and modern materials.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What kind of buildings can you see in this town square?" (Students respond with 1-2 words or short phrases: "modern," "traditional," "school," "market.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "There is a fountain next to the library." (Teacher points to specific locations and states T/F statements, students respond "True" or "False".)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice asking and answering WH-questions about locations, using "There is/are," prepositions of place (e.g., next to, across from, in front of), imperatives (e.g., "Turn left," "Go straight"), and sequencing words. They will use vocabulary such as <em>town square, map, building, traditional, modern, street, park, library, market, fountain</em>. Pronunciation focuses on consonant clusters with /s/ and syllable stress in words like "monument," "tradition."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Choral Repetition):</b> The teacher provides a handout with sentences describing the town square map from Stage 2, but with blanks for prepositions of place or "there is/are." For example: "___ is a tall modern building ___ the corner ___ the main street." Students complete the sentences individually. Then, the teacher leads a choral repetition of the correct sentences, focusing on clear pronunciation, especially of /s/ clusters and syllable stress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Paired Question & Answer Drill):</b> Students work in pairs. Using the same map, Student A asks "Where is the [location]?" (e.g., "Where is the library?"). Student B answers using "There is/are" and prepositions (e.g., "The library is next to the school"). They then switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing feedback on grammar and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction (Directions & Preferences):</b> Still in pairs, Student A describes how to get from one point to another on the map using imperatives and sequencing words (e.g., "First, walk straight ahead. Second, turn left at the fountain. The market is there."). Student B follows the directions on their map. Then, they discuss preferences: "Do you like the traditional market or the modern shopping center? Why?" Student B responds, "I like the [traditional/modern] market because it is [busy/quiet/spacious]." They switch roles.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-play – "Town Square Tour Guides"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups of three. Each group receives two different maps or descriptions of the same town square: one showing how it *used to be* (e.g., "Old Town Square Map") and another showing how it *is now* (e.g., "Modern Town Square Map"). One student acts as a "Tourist" who is new to the town. The other two students act as "Tour Guides" who know the area well. The Tour Guides collaboratively describe the town square, pointing out key locations (e.g., fountain, statue, historic church, modern library building). They must explain directions (e.g., "First, walk to the monument, then turn right"), describe changes (e.g., "The old market used to be small, but now there is a larger, modern market building across the street"), and talk about preferences for places (e.g., "I like the historic plaza because it is quiet and traditional. What do you think, Tourist?"). The Tourist asks WH-questions about specific locations and expresses their preferences. Students switch roles after 3-4 minutes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, use of "There is/are," WH-questions, imperatives, sequencing words, comparative adjectives (e.g., smaller/larger, older/newer), and expressing preferences, all while discussing traditional and modern building materials in the town square scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Town Square Tour Guides" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes student pairs/groups as they perform their "Town Square Tour Guides" role-play. The teacher pays attention to their ability to describe locations, give directions, express preferences, and compare places, using the target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of consonant clusters with /s/ and correct syllable stress in multi-syllable words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Accurately uses nouns (e.g., market, library, monument, building), verbs (e.g., walk, turn, cross), adjectives (e.g., historic, modern, busy), and prepositions of place.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency:</b> Communicates descriptions, directions, and preferences with reasonable clarity and flow, without excessive hesitation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Usage:</b> Correctly uses "There is/are," WH-questions, imperatives, sequencing words, and basic comparative structures to describe changes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and Interaction:</b> Actively participates, listens, and responds appropriately in the group discussion/role-play.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now describe at least three places in a town square using location words: _______________" (e.g., "The park is next to the library. There is a statue in the plaza. The school is across from the market.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to (circle one) _________________ when talking about the town square. I want to practice more with _______________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student self-reflection and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when describing places and giving directions today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do I need more practice with to speak about the town square and its changes?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., Most students actively used "There is/are" and prepositions. The guided interaction helped build confidence.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with sequencing words for directions; provide more visual cues or sentence frames. Ensure consistent use of comparative adjectives when discussing changes.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today by having students write descriptive paragraphs about the town square, its features, and perhaps its changes, incorporating the traditional and modern building materials.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>